onal security adviser Zbigniew Brzezinski . But , he <ei41 notes> , " If everything <ei42 crumbles> totally , that wo n't be such a problem . " American officials staunchly <ei43 disavow> any interest in <ei44 driving> through Iraq toward Baghdad , either in <ei45 pursuit> of Saddam Hussein himself or to <ei46 set> up some American-controlled government inside Iraq . The Americans <ei47 say> their battle plans <ei48 call> for <ei49 operating> against forces inside Iraq as far north as the city of Basra , about 30 miles north of Kuwait , but say there is n't any plan to <ei50 drive> beyond that . Indeed , French President Francois Mitterrand <ei51 said> yesterday that some allied forces are <ei53 crossing> Iraqi territory as part of a " pincer " movement to <ei54 trap> the soldiers occupying Kuwait , but <ei55 insisted> , " The purpose is n't to <ei56 invade> Iraqi territory , that 's not the aim , that is n't the mandate . " Nevertheless , American officials over the weekend <ei58 became> more <ei59 open> in <ei60 declaring> that by <ei62 destroying> Saddam Hussein 's military machine they <ei63 hope> to <ei64 destroy> his regime -- a goal likely to be <ei65 supported> by most Americans . In a pre-attack message , Lt. Gen. Walter Boomer , the top Marine in the Persian Gulf , <ei66 told> U.S. Marines that their goal is to " <ei67 restore> { Kuwait } to its citizens . " He <ei68 went> on to <ei69 add> that " in so <ei70 doing> you not only <ei71 return> a nation to its people , but you will <ei72 destroy> the war machine of a ruthless dictator . " Secretary of State James Baker <ei73 said> on ABC - TV 's " This Week With David Brinkley " that the series of United Nations resolutions <ei74 condemning> Iraq 's <ei75 invasion> of Kuwait " <ei76 imply> that the <ei77 restoration> of peace and stability in the Gulf would be a heck of a lot <ei78 easier> if he and that leadership were not in power in Iraq . " Of course , it is still far too early to <ei79 assume> that the military <ei80 situation> on the ground will <ei81 stay> as smooth for allied forces as it <ei82 appears> to have been so far . Iraq still <ei83 has> the potential to <ei84 cause> significant <ei85 problems> by <ei87 using> forces and weapons that do n't yet <ei88 seem> fully <ei89 engaged> . For one thing , Iraq still apparently has n't <ei90 unleashed> its stockpile of chemical weapons . Gen. Schwarzkopf <ei91 said> that some early reports that chemical weapons were <ei92 used> against allied troops <ei93 turned> out to be " bogus . " Iraq is <ei94 believed> to <ei95 have> the ability to <ei96 deliver> chemical weapons in artillery shells or , perhaps , atop Soviet-made Frog7 missiles . Perhaps more important , it <ei97 appears> that allied troops have n't yet fully <ei98 engaged> Iraq 's vaunted Republican Guard , which has been <ei99 sitting> just north of the Iraq - Kuwait border and is <ei100 considered> the most potent element in the Iraqi defense . It <ei101 remains> to be <ei102 seen> how much <ei103 damage> the allied air <ei104 campaign> was able to <ei105 inflict> on the Guard , and whether President Hussein will <ei106 commit> his most valued troops to a fight-to-the-death finish . Certainly Saddam Hussein <ei107 continues> to <ei108 implore> his country to <ei109 fight> on . " <ei110 Fight> them , " he <ei111 urged> Iraqis in a radio <ei112 address> . " All Iraqis , <ei113 fight> them with all the power you <ei114 have> , and all <ei115 struggle> for everything . " American war planners have long <ei116 assumed> that the early stage of the ground <ei117 attack> , in which American forces would <ei118 use> their speed to <ei119 sweep> around Iraqi defenses and their strength to <ei120 punch> through the relatively weak Iraqi front line , would be the easiest part . Despite these early successes , the mere fact that a ground <ei121 campaign> has <ei122 begun> almost <ei123 guarantees> that the Bush administration will <ei124 face> fresh problems <ei125 growing> out of the military <ei126 situation> . There are likely to be additional American prisoners of war taken , and there are signs that President Hussein is <ei127 taking> Kuwaiti hostages . U.S. and Kuwaiti officials <ei128 say> there are reports that large numbers of civilians from Kuwait City are being <ei129 rounded> up and <ei130 held> by Iraqi troops , apparently either for <ei131 use> as human shields or for <ei132 use> later in <ei133 bargaining> once the <ei134 war> is over . President Bush 's political argument for <ei135 going> to a ground <ei136 war> has been <ei137 strengthened> by the growing stream of reports of wanton Iraqi <ei138 destruction> inside Kuwait . U.S. officials <ei139 say> that hundreds of Kuwaiti oil wells now may have been <ei140 set> afire . And Robert Gates , Mr. Bush 's deputy national security adviser , <ei141 asserted> in an <ei142 interview> on the Cable News Network that Iraqi troops have <ei143 set> fire to " large sections " of Kuwait City . Mr. Bush and his aides were <ei144 leaning> toward a military <ei145 conclusion> of the <ei146 crisis> even before the latest reports of Iraqi <ei147 atrocities> in Kuwait <ei148 came> to light . The president and his top aides tentatively <ei149 decided> on Feb . 11 that a ground <ei151 war> would be <ei152 necessary> . The <ei153 decision> was <ei154 made> after Defense Secretary Dick Cheney and Gen. Colin Powell , chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff , <ei155 returned> from a <ei156 visit> with military commanders in Saudi Arabia , administration officials <ei157 say> . Then , a week or so ago , Gen. Schwarzkopf secretly <ei159 picked> Saturday night as the optimal time to <ei161 start> the <ei162 offensive> . The date was <ei163 unaffected> by the last-ditch Soviet peace <ei164 initiative> . The real problem with the Soviet proposals , U.S. officials now <ei165 say> , was that they all would have <ei166 required> <ei167 lifting> economic <ei168 sanctions> against Iraq . The Bush administration <ei169 considers> the <ei170 sanctions> essential to <ei171 keeping> Saddam Hussein under control should he <ei172 survive> the <ei173 war> . Mr. Bush <ei174 forestalled> further diplomatic <ei175 maneuvering> by <ei177 issuing> an ultimatum on behalf of the allies <ei178 demanding> that Iraq <ei179 withdraw> within a week , <ei181 starting> at noon Saturday . Administration aides <ei183 said> that the idea of the ultimatum was Gen. Powell 's . He <ei184 argued> <ei185 setting> an explicit deadline for Saddam Hussein to <ei186 break> would , when it was <ei187 broken> , <ei188 give> the U.S. military a clear green light to <ei189 proceed> . In <ei190 setting> out his final challenge to Saddam Hussein , Mr. Bush <ei191 continued> the intensive personal <ei192 diplomacy> he <ei193 began> after the <ei194 invasion> last August . After <ei196 cabling> world leaders about his intention to <ei197 give> Saddam Hussein a final deadline to <ei198 exit> Kuwait , he <ei199 offered> him a week to <ei201 withdraw> fully , instead of the four days he originally <ei203 considered> , because of <ei205 objections> from some European partners that four days <ei206 seemed> punitive and unrealistic . And when he and President Gorbachev <ei207 spoke> about the <ei208 decision> in a <ei209 talk> lasting nearly an hour , the President <ei211 took> pains to <ei212 listen> to what his counterpart had to <ei213 say> , although he already had <ei214 decided> that the Soviet alternative to the allied deadline was <ei215 unacceptable> . Finally , when Iraq <ei216 failed> to <ei217 respond> to the U.S. ultimatum , Mr. Bush <ei218 let> the ground <ei219 offensive> <ei220 begin> as previously <ei221 planned> Saturday night . The <ei223 attack> was lightning quick , as allied forces <ei224 punched> through tall sand berms on the border and <ei225 pushed> forward into Iraq and Kuwait . U.S. Marines were <ei226 said> to have <ei227 breached> troublesome mine fields along the Iraqi lines but Pentagon officials <ei228 said> no amphibious <ei229 assault> on Kuwait 's beaches had <ei230 begun> . Long columns of Iraqi prisoners of war could be <ei231 seen> <ei232 trudging> through the desert toward the allied rear . U.S. commanders <ei233 said> 5,500 Iraqi prisoners were <ei234 taken> in the first hours of the ground <ei236 war> , though some military officials later <ei237 said> the total may have <ei238 climbed> above 8,000 . The U.S. <ei239 hopes> its troops will <ei240 drive> Iraqi forces out of Kuwait quickly , <ei241 leaving> much of Iraq 's offensive military equipment <ei242 destroyed> or <ei243 abandoned> in Kuwait . It <ei244 expects> that tens of thousands of Iraqi soldiers will <ei245 surrender> to the U.S. and its allies over the next few days . If the allies <ei247 succeed> , Saddam Hussein will have <ei248 plunged> his country first into a fruitless eight-year <ei250 war> against Iran and then into a humiliating <ei251 war> against the U.S. and the allies to <ei252 defend> his <ei253 conquest> of Kuwait , <ei254 leaving> much of his country 's military establishment and modern infrastructure in ruins . Meanwhile , the U.S. <ei255 hopes> , economic <ei256 sanctions> and an international arms <ei257 embargo> will <ei258 remain> in effect until Iraq <ei259 pays> war reparations to Kuwait to <ei260 cover> war damages . That would <ei261 undermine> any chances of <ei262 rebuilding> either Iraq or its armed forces in short order as long as Saddam Hussein <ei263 remains> in power . The American hope is that someone from within Iraq , perhaps from the army 's professional ranks , will step forward and push Saddam Hussein aside so that the country can begin recovering from the disaster . Outside analysts think Saddam Hussein 's position is indeed precarious . " I think frankly Saddam is <ei264 finished> , no matter what <ei265 happens> , " <ei266 says> Christine Helms , a Middle East scholar who has <ei267 written> extensively about Iraq . " These guys simply do n't <ei268 retire> to condos over the Euphrates . " Despite the lack of any obvious successors , the Iraqi leader 's internal power base <ei269 appeared> to be <ei270 narrowing> even before the <ei271 war> <ei272 began> . Some analysts <ei273 say> he <ei274 appeared> to be <ei275 relying> on a smaller and smaller circle of close advisers and relatives . If that 's true , the <ei276 narrowing> of his support would <ei277 make> it easier for someone to <ei278 push> him aside from within . Yet , paradoxically , the perception that the U.S. <ei279 wants> to <ei280 destroy> Iraq may <ei281 increase> Saddam Hussein 's <ei282 support> within the Iraqi military . And the U.S. now will <ei283 face> sharper questions in the Arab world since it did n't <ei284 back> the peace proposals worked out in Moscow . " We <ei285 looked> to the United States , we <ei286 expected> you to <ei287 have> the moral edge , " <ei288 says> Nasser Tahboub , a Jerusalem-born Jordanian who <ei289 has> an American wife and a doctorate in political science from Duke University . " Now we <ei290 see> that edge <ei291 eroded> . For me , it is a great <ei292 tragedy> . For the first time in history , the U.S. has <ei293 gone> to <ei294 war> with an Arab and Muslim nation , and we <ei295 know> a peaceful solution was in reach . " -- Geraldine Brooks in Amman , Jordan , and Craig Forman in Cairo , Egypt , contributed to this article .

